<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Q'eqchi' people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kekchi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Q'eqchi' are a Maya people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alta-verapaz">Alta Verapaz</a> and spreading into Izabal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> and southern Belize. Their language, Q'eqchi', is one of the most widely spoken Mayan languages, with more than a million speakers. From the late nineteenth century much of their land was taken for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations, and many migrated north into the lowland forests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qeqchi-people.jpg" alt="Q'eqchi' people" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Infrogmation of New Orleans · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CobanAVPJovenes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Q'eqchi' trace their beginnings to a large recorded migration from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> towards the Guatemalan highlands, where they settled as a sedentary society growing corn; their pre-Hispanic development took place in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alta-verapaz">Alta Verapaz</a>. Before the Spanish conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> began in the 1520s, their settlements lay in the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Alta Verapaz and Baja Verapaz. Their land was known as Tezulutlan, the land of war, and they were ruled by a king with laws and a government of their own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Their dispersed population made them hard for the Spanish to control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> was allowed to try to convert them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, but only a small part were converted, and the church lost its hold over them, which left them open to exploitation by plantation owners and slavers. In the 19th century plantations seized their communal land and made them work it. In 1877 the government abolished all communal landownership, and some Q'eqchi' moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a>.</p><p>Displacements, resettlements, persecutions and migrations over the centuries spread their communities into Izabal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> and El Quiché, the Toledo District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a> and, in smaller numbers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Today Q'eqchi' speakers are the most widely spread of all the Maya peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditionally the Q'eqchi' believe in the Tzuultaq'a, male and female guardian deities who dwell in the caves of the local mountains and preside over nature, beliefs they have blended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Their religious specialists include the aj ilonel, a curer with herbs and ceremonies, the aj q'e, a seer, and the aj tuul, a sorcerer. The dead are wrapped in a petate, a palm mat, and buried with what they will need on the journey to the afterlife.</p><p>Farming is mostly for the family's own needs. From the 1880s to around the 1940s plantation owners forbade the growing of any crops but corn and beans, which left a diet that depends on corn. Planting, cultivating and harvesting are rituals in which farmers see themselves working alongside their God. Marriages are arranged by the parents, and property usually goes to the child who offers to care for them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qʼeqchiʼ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Guatemala has the largest population in Central America, about 18 million people, and the largest indigenous share: speakers of K’iche’, <b>Q’eqchi</b>’, Kaqchikel, Mam and some twenty other Mayan languages make up over 40 per cent of the population in census figures, alongside a ladino majority, Garífuna and Xinca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
